"""Small, question-specific scaffolds; hints never require another page."""
# Each entry: plain-language task, starting hint, then a sequence of reasoning steps.
# Difficulty audit 2026-09 (DIFFICULTY_AUDIT_2026-09.md) relabelled these scaffolded items
# below Hard; their scaffolds are kept deliberately. Written: 30, 62, 92, 96, 166, 167,
# 168, 201, 204, 205 (now Medium). MCQ: 19, 40, 59, 60, 79, 80, 99, 119, 120 (Medium);
# 20, 39, 100 (Easy).
WRITTEN = {
27: ('Show that two eventual upper bounds can be chained.', 'Write the two inequalities and choose a point where both are valid.', ['Transitive means that f being bounded by g, and g by h, also bounds f by h.', 'Beyond n₁, f ≤ c₁g. Beyond n₂, g ≤ c₂h. Choose n ≥ max(n₁,n₂).', 'Substitute the second inequality into the first: f ≤ c₁c₂h. The new constant is c₁c₂.']),
28: ('Compare a logarithm with even a tiny positive power.', 'Replace n by 2ᵐ so the logarithm becomes m.', ['Take logarithms in base 2; another fixed base changes only a constant.', 'Now log₂ n = m and nᵏ = 2^(km). Since k > 0, this is an exponential with base greater than 1.', 'When m doubles, the linear part merely doubles, while the exponential part is squared. Repeating this makes m / 2^(km) tend to zero. A tiny k delays this behaviour but does not change the eventual result.']),
29: ('Find the least travel needed to visit both ends of a line and return.', 'Draw the two extreme points. A closed trip has to cross their separation in both directions.', ['Let L be the leftmost point, R the rightmost, and S the start, with L ≤ S ≤ R.', 'Any closed trip includes a part from L to R and another from R to L. Each part costs at least R − L.', 'The route S → L → R → S costs (S−L)+(R−L)+(R−S)=2(R−L). One scan finds L and R.']),
30: ('Show why choosing the earliest finish can lose money.', 'Try two overlapping jobs: one short and cheap, one long and valuable.', ['Give A the interval [1,2) and fee 1; give B [1,10) and fee 100.', 'Earliest finish selects A and must reject B because they overlap.', 'The better solution selects B alone. One counterexample disproves the rule for fees; it does not disprove every greedy method.']),
31: ('Estimate 1 + 1/2 + … + 1/n without adding each term.', 'Group denominators between consecutive powers of two.', ['For n = 8, use blocks 1; 1/2; 1/3+1/4; 1/5+…+1/8.', 'A block ending at 2ᵏ has 2^(k−1) terms, each between 1/2ᵏ and 1/2^(k−1). Its sum lies between 1/2 and 1.', 'There are about log₂ n such blocks. Constant work per block gives matching upper and lower bounds proportional to log n. A final incomplete block does not change that growth.']),
59: ('Explain why left–root–right prints a search tree in order.', 'Assume each smaller subtree already prints correctly.', ['For an empty tree, the output is empty and sorted.', 'For distinct keys, every left-subtree key is below the root and every right-subtree key is above it.', 'By induction, each subtree output is sorted. Joining sorted-left, root, sorted-right creates a sorted whole.']),
60: ('Support fast deletion without leaving holes in the array.', 'The array need not stay sorted: replace a removed item with the last one.', ['Store [A,B,C] and a map A→0, B→1, C→2.', 'Delete B by moving C into slot 1, changing C→1, and popping the old last slot. Remove B from the map.', 'A uniform random index selects each stored key equally often. Hashing is expected constant time; resizing makes the array cost amortised, meaning averaged over a sequence of operations.']),
61: ('Keep the smallest stack value available without searching.', 'Remember the minimum after every push.', ['Push 5,3,7. The main stack is [5,3,7]; the minimum stack is [5,3,3].', 'Each minimum entry describes the main-stack prefix ending at the same depth.', 'Pop both stacks together. The new minimum is still at the top. On the first push use x itself; handle empty-stack queries explicitly.']),
62: ('Explain why a deleted hash slot must not look unused.', 'Searching stops at a never-used slot, but must continue past a deleted one.', ['The four inserted keys occupy slots 0,1,2,3 respectively.', 'If slot 1 becomes ordinary empty, searching for 21 stops there and misses slot 2.', 'A tombstone means “used before, keep searching.” Insertion may reuse it, but must still check for an existing duplicate key if the interface is a set.']),
63: ('Evict the item that has gone longest without a recent use.', 'Use a hash map to find an item and a linked list to record recency.', ['The list front is most recent; the back is least recent. Map each key directly to its list node.', 'For a successful get or an update, detach the node and move it to the front using its two links.', 'For a new item at capacity, remove the back node and its map entry. Hash lookup is expected O(1); list changes are O(1). Define the k=0 case as storing nothing.']),
64: ('Use a hypothetical faster dictionary to build an impossibly fast sort.', 'Insert everything, then repeatedly remove the smallest.', ['Little-o(log n) means a cost whose ratio to log n tends to zero, not just a small constant factor.', 'If both operations had that worst-case cost, n insertions and n removals would use o(n log n) comparisons.', 'The removals print sorted order. This contradicts the comparison-sorting lower bound, so the two assumed bounds cannot both hold.']),
89: ('Trap the logarithm of n! between two n log n expressions.', 'For the lower bound, keep only the largest half of the factors.', ['Upper bound: each of the n factors is at most n, so n! ≤ nⁿ.', 'Lower bound: at least n/2 factors are at least n/2, so n! ≥ (n/2)^(n/2), ignoring harmless integer rounding.', 'Taking logs gives (n/2)log₂(n/2) ≤ log₂(n!) ≤ n log₂ n. Both sides grow as n log n.']),
90: ('Count how many values must be ruled out as the maximum.', 'Every value except the winner must lose at least once.', ['Initially each of n distinct values could be the maximum.', 'A comparison rules out at most one new candidate: its loser.', 'To leave one candidate, at least n−1 candidates must be ruled out. A running-maximum scan meets this bound.']),
91: ('Sort bounded integers using two digits instead of pairwise comparisons.', 'Write x as high·n + low.', ['For 0 ≤ x < n², high = floor(x/n) and low = x mod n, each in 0,…,n−1.', 'Stable counting sort first by low, then by high. Stability preserves the low-digit order within a high-digit tie.', 'Each pass scans n items and n counters: O(n). Two passes remain O(n), assuming the integers fit in a constant number of machine words.']),
92: ('Use the unused end of the same array as the sorted output.', 'A max-heap already puts the largest remaining item at its root.', ['Build a max-heap in the array.', 'Swap its root with the last active slot; that slot now contains its final sorted value.', 'Shorten the active heap, then bubble the new root down. Repeat; the sorted suffix grows and needs no second array.']),
93: ('Keep the middle values at the tops of two heaps.', 'Separate the smaller half from the larger half.', ['The smaller half is a max-heap, so its largest item is visible. The larger half is a min-heap, so its smallest is visible.', 'After insertion, move a root across if the sizes differ by more than one. Keep every lower-half value ≤ every upper-half value.', 'For odd size, report the larger heap’s root. For even size, report the mean of both roots. Each insertion needs only a constant number of O(log n) heap operations.']),
94: ('Repeatedly choose the smallest of the current list heads.', 'Only one candidate from each list needs to be in the heap.', ['Put the first item of each nonempty list into a min-heap, remembering its list.', 'Remove the smallest, output it, then add the next item from the same list.', 'The heap never exceeds k entries. Each of n outputs costs O(log k); bottom-up heap construction costs O(k).']),
95: ('Measure how many out-of-order pairs an adjacent swap can fix.', 'Start with a reverse-sorted array of distinct keys.', ['An inversion is a pair of positions i < j whose values satisfy a[i] > a[j].', 'Reverse order contains n(n−1)/2 inversions.', 'Swapping neighbours changes only their mutual order, removing at most one inversion. Reaching zero therefore needs Ω(n²) swaps in this case.']),
96: ('Trace what happens when the partition never finds a smaller key.', 'With all keys equal, every strict “less than pivot” test fails.', ['The pivot moves to one end and the remaining subproblem has n−1 keys.', 'Work is proportional to n+(n−1)+…+1, so it is quadratic.', 'Three-way partition collects equal keys into a middle block. No recursion is needed on that block; all-equal data is handled in one linear scan.']),
97: ('Pair very small values with very large values to control the worst pair.', 'Compare an arbitrary pairing with one that pairs the two extremes.', ['Suppose the smallest a pairs with x and the largest z pairs with y.', 'Replace these pairs by (a,z) and (x,y). Since a ≤ y and x ≤ z, both new sums are at most the old sum y+z.', 'The maximum cannot increase. Fix the extremes together and apply the same argument to the remaining values. This is an exchange argument: improve the structure without worsening the objective.']),
127: ('Explain why an undirected DFS edge cannot jump between finished branches.', 'DFS examines every neighbour before finishing a vertex.', ['Let x be discovered before y, with edge {x,y}.', 'If y is still undiscovered when this edge is examined, DFS descends toward y before finishing x.', 'Thus the two endpoints lie on an ancestor–descendant chain. A non-tree edge is viewed upward as a back edge; its reverse is not a second edge.']),
128: ('Explain why a BFS parent chain uses the fewest edges.', 'Think of the queue as distance layers 0,1,2,… .', ['The start is layer 0. Its newly discovered neighbours form layer 1.', 'All layer-d vertices leave the queue before any layer-(d+1) vertex is processed.', 'A vertex first reached from layer d cannot have a shorter undiscovered route: that route’s earlier layer would already have found it. Its parent chain therefore has shortest length d+1.']),
129: ('Connect two-colouring with even and odd cycles.', 'Prove both directions separately.', ['If an edge always changes colour, a cycle returns to its starting colour only after an even number of edges.', 'Conversely, colour BFS levels alternately in each component.', 'An edge within the same level would create an odd cycle: take the two tree branches from their last common ancestor, then that edge. With no odd cycle, every edge joins opposite colours.']),
130: ('Show that two different cheapest trees cannot coexist with distinct weights.', 'Exchange one carefully chosen edge between the two trees.', ['Choose the lightest edge e that appears in exactly one proposed MST, say T₁.', 'Adding e to T₂ creates a cycle. That cycle contains an edge f outside T₁; otherwise T₁ would contain a cycle too.', 'Because weights are distinct and e was lightest among differing edges, f is heavier. Replacing f by e makes T₂ cheaper, a contradiction.']),
131: ('Show that an MST also makes its largest edge as small as possible.', 'Remove a largest MST edge and look at the two resulting parts.', ['Call that edge weight B. Suppose another spanning tree uses only edges lighter than B.', 'That other tree must cross the cut between the two parts using some edge f of weight below B.', 'Substitute f for the removed edge: the original MST would become cheaper. The contradiction proves no spanning tree has a smaller bottleneck.']),
132: ('Find shortest paths by processing a DAG in dependency order.', 'A topological order puts every edge’s start before its end.', ['Set the source distance to 0 and the others to infinity.', 'At each reachable vertex u, try dist[u]+w(u,v) for every outgoing edge and keep improvements.', 'All possible predecessors are already processed, so a negative edge causes no late return. Visit each vertex and edge once: O(n+m) word operations.']),
133: ('Find the cheapest tree that differs from a given MST.', 'Add one unused edge, then remove an edge from its newly formed cycle.', ['Each non-tree edge e creates exactly one cycle with T.', 'Remove a heaviest edge on that cycle to get the cheapest tree obtainable with this e. Record its weight, even if tied with T.', 'Try every non-tree edge and keep the best. A standard tree-exchange argument guarantees a best alternative among these swaps. Finding each tree path by a scan costs O(n), for O(nm) overall.']),
134: ('Remove one leaf, use the smaller tree, then add the leaf back.', 'A finite tree with at least two vertices has a leaf.', ['For one vertex there are zero edges, matching n−1.', 'Remove a leaf and its single incident edge. Remaining vertices still form a tree.', 'By induction that smaller tree has n−2 edges. Restoring one edge gives n−1.']),
161: ('Compare the number of recursive calls with the Fibonacci value.', 'Count the current call as well as its two recursive branches.', ['Set C(0)=C(1)=1. For n≥2, C(n)=1+C(n−1)+C(n−2).', 'Assuming C(n−1)≥F(n−1) and C(n−2)≥F(n−2), their sum already reaches F(n).', 'The extra current call can only increase the count. Both base cases hold, so induction proves C(n)≥F(n), an exponential lower bound.']),
162: ('Change DAG shortest-path minimisation into maximisation.', 'Use minus infinity for an unreachable vertex so it cannot win a maximum.', ['Set long[s]=0, all other values to −∞, and process topological order.', 'For each reachable u, update long[v]=max(long[v],long[u]+w(u,v)).', 'With cycles, a simple path must remember used vertices to avoid repetition. A state containing only the current vertex loses that information.']),
163: ('Add a local case for swapping the last two characters.', 'Only use the new case when those characters match in reverse order.', ['Suppose s ends in …ab and t ends in …ba at the prefixes being compared.', 'Besides insertion, deletion and substitution, allow the cost for the prefixes two characters shorter, plus 1.', 'This restricted recurrence gives optimal string alignment distance. It does not allow every combination of overlapping edits that unrestricted Damerau–Levenshtein permits.']),
164: ('Find a good split, then prove every smaller cap fails.', 'Test the cap 13 by packing each positive range as far right as possible.', ['A cap of 14 works: [3,1,4,1] | [5,9] | [2,6], with sums 9,14,8.', 'With cap 13, maximal packing gives [3,1,4,1] | [5] | [9,2] | [6]: four ranges.', 'For positive entries, ending any earlier range sooner cannot improve how far the same number of ranges reaches. So three ranges cannot fit under 13, proving optimum 14.']),
165: ('Balance the number of remaining tests after an egg breaks.', 'With one egg left, you must test upwards one floor at a time.', ['Try floors 4,7,9,10 while the first egg survives: the jumps are 4,3,2,1.', 'If it breaks, search the skipped floors with the remaining egg. The worst branch still totals only four drops.', 'With three drops, two eggs cover at most 3+2+1=6 floors. Four is therefore both sufficient and necessary for ten floors.']),
166: ('Count the states rather than assuming DP means polynomial.', 'Remember both the current city and the set already visited.', ['A set of n cities has 2ⁿ subsets. The current city adds at most n possibilities.', 'Each state considers at most n previous cities or next transitions, depending on the formulation.', 'Multiplying gives O(n²2ⁿ). Reusing states beats enumerating n! tours, but there are still exponentially many states.']),
167: ('Make the largest first set leave two awkward uncovered elements.', 'Give those two elements different remaining sets.', ['S₁ covers four elements, so greedy takes it first.', 'Element 5 then requires S₂, and element 6 requires S₃: three sets total.', 'But S₂∪S₃ already equals the whole universe. Two sets suffice, while no single listed set covers it.']),
168: ('Avoid Sudoku choices already ruled out by a row, column or box.', 'A cell’s candidates are the missing digits from all three constraints.', ['Collect the digits used in the cell’s row, column and 3×3 box.', 'Offer only 1,…,9 minus that union. Zero candidates means this branch cannot succeed.', 'Choose an empty cell with the fewest candidates next. This is a heuristic for finding conflicts early, not a guarantee of polynomial search.']),
169: ('For each job, compare taking it with skipping it.', 'Sort by finish time, then find the last compatible earlier job.', ['Let p(i) be the last earlier job ending no later than job i starts; use half-open intervals.', 'Skipping earns OPT(i−1). Taking earns feeᵢ+OPT(p(i)), because only that compatible prefix remains available.', 'Set OPT(0)=0 and keep the larger amount. Sorting and binary-searching all predecessors cost O(n log n); filling the table costs O(n).']),
170: ('Track which target sums can be reached using a prefix of the items.', 'For an item s, a sum t either skips s or comes from t−s.', ['Initially only sum 0 is reachable with no items.', 'Use can[i,t]=can[i−1,t] OR can[i−1,t−sᵢ], with the second branch allowed only if t≥sᵢ.', 'There are (n+1)(T+1) cells. A target such as T=2ᵇ has only b+1 binary digits but needs roughly 2ᵇ columns. That is why O(nT) is pseudo-polynomial.']),
199: ('Turn a target-sum question into an equal-halves question.', 'Create two large items that must go into different halves.', ['Original total W plus new weights 2W−t and W+t gives total 4W; each half must weigh 2W.', 'The two new weights together are 3W, so they cannot share a half.', 'The half containing 2W−t needs exactly t from the original items. This proves both directions, and the numbers have polynomial bit length. Finally, checking a proposed partition is polynomial, giving NP membership.']),
200: ('Close any all-vertex path by adding one universal vertex.', 'A universal vertex is adjacent to every original vertex.', ['For an undirected graph with at least two vertices, add z and edges from z to all original vertices.', 'An original Hamiltonian path u→…→v becomes the cycle z→u→…→v→z.', 'Conversely, delete z from any Hamiltonian cycle of the new graph to get a path through every old vertex. Tiny inputs can be handled separately; a cycle certificate is polynomial to check.']),
201: ('See why two colours force choices but three colours do not.', 'After colouring one vertex, count its neighbour’s available colours.', ['With two colours, each neighbour has just one allowed colour.', 'With three, each neighbour may have two allowed colours, and choices can interact across distant edges.', 'Thus the forced BFS procedure no longer solves every case. This explains why that argument fails, not why every possible algorithm must be exponential.']),
202: ('Cover both ends of disjoint edges and compare with any optimum.', 'Each chosen edge forces every cover to include at least one endpoint.', ['Pick an uncovered edge, add both endpoints, and discard every edge now covered. Continue until none remain.', 'Chosen edges have no shared endpoints, so they form a matching of size r. Every cover needs at least r vertices.', 'Your cover uses exactly 2r vertices, hence at most twice optimum. “Maximal” means no more disjoint edges can be added; it need not be the largest matching.']),
203: ('Compare an MST walk with the unknown best tour.', 'An optimal tour with one edge removed is a spanning tree.', ['Therefore the MST weight is at most the optimal tour length.', 'Traverse each tree edge in both directions: a closed walk of length 2·MST visits every vertex.', 'Skip repeated vertices. Triangle inequality ensures a direct shortcut is no longer than the replaced detour. The resulting tour costs at most 2·OPT.']),
204: ('Distinguish showing that one assignment works from showing none work.', 'A failed candidate does not rule out the other candidates.', ['A satisfying assignment is a short witness: check every clause.', 'For unsatisfiability, no universal polynomial-size certificate with a polynomial-time checker is known; restricted cases may still have easy proofs.', 'UNSAT belongs to co-NP. Whether it also belongs to NP is unresolved and equivalent to NP=co-NP. Do not turn “unknown” into “impossible.”']),
205: ('Count the small building blocks, then cover their edges.', 'Use one vertex from each variable pair and two from each clause triangle.', ['There are 3 variable edges: 6 vertices. Two clause triangles add 6 more, giving 12; the target is 3+2·2=7.', 'Select variable vertices x,y,z. In triangle (x,y,¬z), leave x out and select y,¬z. In triangle (¬x,y,z), leave y out and select ¬x,z.', 'Each variable edge is covered; two vertices cover every triangle edge. A skipped clause vertex has its same-literal variable endpoint selected, so its cross edge is covered too.']),
}
MCQ = {
19: ('Compare eventual growth, not values at one small input.', 'Any fixed positive power of n eventually beats any fixed power of log n.', ['Option (c) reverses this rule: (log n)²/√n tends to zero.', 'The other comparisons follow the ladder: logarithms, powers, exponentials, then factorials.']),
20: ('Find the full left-to-right spread and count a return trip.', 'A closed route must cross the spread in both directions.', ['The spread is 9−(−10)=19.', 'The lower bound is 2·19=38. Route 0→−10→9→0 reaches it.']),
39: ('Trace each insertion into the first available slot.', 'Start at k mod 5 and move right if occupied.', ['10 takes 0; 15 probes 0 and takes 1; 3 takes 3.', '20 probes 0, then 1, then finds 2 empty. So the answer is slot 2.']),
40: ('Separate location-based operations from ordered searches.', 'An ordinary hash table does not keep keys in numeric order.', ['A known linked-list node can be spliced out directly; a located sorted-array key has its predecessor next door; a BST search follows one height-h path.', 'The hash table must examine stored keys to find the minimum unless it maintains extra information. That makes (d) false.']),
59: ('Count digits in base n instead of base ten.', 'The biggest allowed integer is just below n³.', ['Write x=d₂n²+d₁n+d₀, with each digit in 0,…,n−1.', 'Three stable counting-sort passes handle d₀, d₁, d₂. Each costs O(n+n), so the total is O(n).']),
60: ('Distinguish expected cost from worst-case cost.', 'Random choices can be unlucky even when their average is good.', ['Balanced pivot splits give about log n levels, with linear work per level.', 'An unlucky sequence of extreme pivots leaves n−1,n−2,… items. Summing those costs gives Θ(n²), so (d) is false.']),
79: ('Trace the vertex that is settled too early.', 'Compare direct A→B with the later route through C.', ['After A, tentative distances are B=2 and C=5. Settled-once Dijkstra freezes B at 2.', 'The route A→C→B costs 5−4=1. A later negative edge improved a supposedly final distance.']),
80: ('Compare the cheapest whole network with the cheapest single journey.', 'Use a triangle with weights 2,2,3.', ['The MST selects the two weight-2 edges, total 4.', 'Between the end vertices, the direct weight-3 edge is a shorter path. Minimising total network weight does not preserve every shortest journey.']),
99: ('Count permutations that move every item.', 'Use D(n)=(n−1)(D(n−1)+D(n−2)).', ['Item 1 has n−1 possible new positions. If its partner moves back to position 1, derange the remaining n−2 items; otherwise contracting that link leaves a derangement problem on n−1 items. This gives (n−1)(D(n−2)+D(n−1)).', 'D(1)=0 and D(2)=1 give D(3)=2 and D(4)=9. For three items, the two possibilities are 231 and 312.', 'Then D(5)=4(9+2)=44. A derangement moves every item, not merely some items.']),
100: ('Count the information a TSP subproblem must remember.', 'Current city alone cannot tell which cities remain.', ['There are at most 2ⁿ visited sets and n current cities.', 'Trying at most n transitions per state gives O(n²2ⁿ). Memoisation avoids repeats but cannot remove the exponential number of sets.']),
119: ('Force a target sum inside one equal half.', 'Find the new total before deciding where the large items can go.', ['Total W+(2W−t)+(W+t)=4W, so each half is 2W.', 'The large items sum to 3W and must be separated. The half containing 2W−t needs precisely t from the original items.']),
120: ('Use a matching as a lower bound on any vertex cover.', 'No one vertex can cover two disjoint matching edges.', ['For r disjoint edges, every cover needs at least r vertices.', 'Taking both endpoints uses 2r. Maximality ensures no uncovered edge remains, so this is a valid cover with the factor-2 guarantee.']),
}
